From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ios-blink-sideload <noreply@example.invalid>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 23:28:51 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] iOS sideload: request desktop site

Chrome's "Request Desktop Site" did nothing: it calls
NavigationManager::ReloadWithUserAgentType(), which was
NOTIMPLEMENTED() for Blink, and items always said they were mobile.

Do it as Chrome on Android does
(NavigationControllerAndroid::SetUseDesktopUserAgent()): the first time,
give the WebContents a desktop user agent override (Chrome on the
Mac's, with client hints to match, where Android uses Linux's); mark
the last committed entry as overriding the user agent, have content
recompute the page's preferences, and load the entry's original request
URL again. Content ignores the viewport meta tag on entries that
override the user agent (WebContentsImpl::ComputeWebPreferences()), so
the page lays out at a desktop width (980 px), zoomed out to fit, like
a page without the tag. Without the recompute, which the commit doesn't
do because the entry already has the flag, a responsive page kept its
phone layout under the Mac user agent. Links and addresses opened from
it keep the override (content inherits it), until Request Mobile Site.
An item's user agent type is now its entry's override flag, so the menu
offers the other mode, and a restored session keeps it (it was saved
but not restored). navigator.platform says "MacIntel" in desktop mode,
as in Safari, instead of "iPhone".

Safari on the iPhone keeps honoring the viewport tag in desktop mode (it
changes only the user agent and navigator.platform), so responsive
sites stay mobile there; this follows Chrome on Android instead.

Status: verified at Chromium 3313f827 in the iOS 27 simulator, with a
test page that has a width=device-width viewport and reports the
User-Agent header, navigator.userAgent, navigator.platform,
navigator.userAgentData and the layout width: Request Desktop Site
reloads it with Chrome on the Mac's user agent, mobile false and
platform macOS in the client hints, navigator.platform MacIntel and a
980 px layout zoomed out to 0.38; reloading, following a link and
restarting the app keep it; the menu then offers Request Mobile Site,
which goes back to the iPhone user agent and a 375 px layout. On real
sites, github.com gets its desktop layout and bilibili.com goes from
m.bilibili.com to the desktop www.bilibili.com. Pinch zoom on the
zoomed-out page is not checked (the simulator tools here have no
two-finger input).

diff --git a/ios/web/content/BUILD.gn b/ios/web/content/BUILD.gn
